# Code comments (agent-written code only)

## Tuning knob

**Change this single value to adjust comment density and length across the scale below.**

```yaml
comment_level: 5   # 0–10
```

| Level | Behavior |
|-------|----------|
| **0** | No comments on code you write or change. |
| **1–2** | Almost none — only when logic is genuinely non-obvious and a name alone cannot convey intent. |
| **3–4** | Light — one short line on tricky branches, algorithms, or non-obvious “why”. |
| **5** | **Default balance** — one-liners on moderately complex logic; skip obvious code. |
| **6–7** | Moderate — comment most non-trivial blocks; still one line each, no paragraphs. |
| **8–9** | Heavy — frequent short comments; multi-line only when several lines need one shared note. |
| **10** | Very verbose — many comments, including longer multi-sentence blocks (avoid unless you want this). |

Apply the row that matches `comment_level`. When in doubt between two rows, prefer the lighter one.

## Scope

These rules apply only to **code you add or modify** in the current task. Do **not** add comments to untouched existing code.

## Preserve existing comments

- Do **not** remove an existing comment if it still accurately describes the code.
- **Update** a comment when you change the code it describes so it stays accurate.
- Remove a comment **only** when the associated code is removed.

## What to comment (at or above your `comment_level`)

- Add **one-line** comments when a concept may be hard to grasp from names alone: non-obvious algorithms, business rules, workarounds, performance or concurrency choices, subtle edge cases.
- Do **not** comment every line, restate what the code obviously does, or label trivial steps (`// increment i`).
- Prefer a short `// why` over a long `// what`.

## What to avoid (at any level below 10)

- No long paragraphs or essay-style blocks.
- No over-explaining simple assignments, getters, or standard patterns the codebase already uses.
- No comment noise that duplicates the code.

## Examples

```typescript
// ❌ Removed useful comment while refactoring
// Was: // Retry once — upstream occasionally returns 503 during deploy
await fetchWithRetry(url);

// ✅ Kept and updated
// Retry once — deploy window still returns intermittent 503s
await fetchWithRetry(url, { maxAttempts: 2 });

// ❌ Paragraph (too long at most levels)
// This function takes the user ID and looks it up in the cache first because
// we found that 80% of requests hit cache and database round-trips add latency...

// ✅ One-liner at level ~5
// Cache-first — most reads hit Redis; DB is fallback
const user = await getUserCached(id);

// ❌ Commenting untouched existing code you didn't change
// (adding comments elsewhere in the file to "improve" unrelated functions)

// ✅ Only comment the block you wrote or edited
```
